    <h1>5 Essential Destinations for Your First Vietnam Holiday</h1>
    <p>Vietnam rewards travellers who look beyond its headline cities. Within a single trip, you can weave through centuries-old alleyways, kayak among towering limestone karsts, cycle past emerald rice paddies, and drift through the floating markets of the Mekong River.</p>
    <p>Stretching over 1,600 kilometres from north to south, the country shifts dramatically in climate, cuisine, and culture from one region to the next. If you are planning your first visit, these five destinations strike the right balance between iconic highlights and places that reveal the real rhythm of daily life here.</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Hanoi: Soak Up the Capital’s Living History</h2>
    <p>Most travellers use Hanoi simply as a launchpad, but rushing through the capital means missing its layered charm. From the chaotic energy of the Old Quarter to the quiet, tree-lined boulevards of the French Quarter, this is a city that reveals itself slowly.</p>
    <p>Start your morning around Hoan Kiem Lake just after sunrise, when locals gather for tai chi, badminton, and brisk walks before the motorbike traffic builds. Just north of the lake lies the Old Quarter, a tangle of narrow lanes where each street was historically dedicated to a single guild, from silver smithing to silk trading. Many streets still carry those trade names today.</p>
    <p>To understand the country's deeper history, visit the Temple of Literature, Vietnam’s first national university, and the Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum complex. Between sights, let the street food guide your route: grab a steaming bowl of <em>pho</em> for breakfast, sit on a low plastic stool for smoky <em>bun cha</em> at lunch, and wind down in a hidden alleyway café with a rich, velvety egg coffee.</p>
    <p><strong>Local tip:</strong> Leave your itinerary loose. Hanoi is best experienced by wandering without a map and watching the city’s sidewalk life unfold.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Lan Ha Bay: Cruise a Quieter Limestone Seascape</h2>
    <p>Tucked just south of famous Ha Long Bay and wrapping around Cat Ba Island, Lan Ha Bay delivers the same dramatic seascape, featuring hundreds of jungle-clad limestone pillars rising from emerald waters, without the heavy boat traffic.</p>
    <p>Because it remains slightly off the main tourist radar, Lan Ha feels far more intimate. You’ll pass floating fishing villages, sheltered lagoons, and pockets of sandy beaches tucked beneath sheer cliffs.</p>
    <p>An overnight cruise is the best way to experience it. Spend your afternoon kayaking through sea caves and swimming in quiet coves, then head to the top deck at dusk as the karst peaks turn into silhouettes against the sunset. While a one-night cruise gives you a great taste of the bay, booking two nights lets you venture deeper into the archipelago’s uncrowded corners.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Hoi An: Step Inside a Lantern-Lit Trading Port</h2>
    <p>Between the 15th and 19th centuries, Hoi An was a bustling international port drawing merchants from Japan, China, and Europe. That blended heritage is preserved in the Ancient Town’s mustard-yellow shophouses, ornate Chinese assembly halls, and the iconic Japanese Covered Bridge.</p>
    <p>Because motorbikes are banned from the centre for much of the day, the Ancient Town is a dream to explore on foot. Beyond the heritage streets, Hoi An is an ideal place to catch your breath mid-trip. Rent a bicycle to ride through the surrounding water-coconut groves and organic herb villages, join a market-to-table cooking class, or spend a lazy afternoon at An Bang Beach.</p>
    <p>Hoi An also punches well above its weight as a food destination. Be sure to try <em>cao lau</em> (thick noodles made with water from ancient local wells), turmeric-tinted <em>mi quang</em>, and delicate white rose dumplings. Stay in town past sunset: as dusk settles, thousands of silk lanterns flicker on along the Thu Bon River, transforming the streets into something out of a storybook.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Hue: Uncover Vietnam’s Imperial Past</h2>
    <p>Just a few hours north of Hoi An over the scenic Hai Van Pass, Hue offers a completely different chapter of Vietnamese history. Set along the banks of the Perfume River, this was the imperial capital of the Nguyen Dynasty from 1802 to 1945.</p>
    <p>At the heart of the city sits the Imperial City, a vast walled citadel of palaces, temples, and ceremonial courtyards. Though heavily damaged during wartime, careful restoration has preserved its regal atmosphere. Out in the wooded hills beyond the city centre lie the elaborate royal tombs of emperors Minh Mang, Tu Duc, and Khai Dinh, each reflecting the distinct architecture and personality of the ruler buried there.</p>
    <p>Hue’s royal legacy lives on in its kitchen, too. Court chefs once competed to create dishes that were as visually balanced as they were flavourful, a tradition you’ll still see in local banquets and street stalls alike. Don't leave without trying <em>bun bo Hue</em>, a deeply savoury, lemongrass-spiced beef noodle soup. Give yourself at least two full days here so you can explore the countryside tombs without rushing.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. The Mekong Delta: Drift Through River Life</h2>
    <p>In the far south, the Mekong River fans out into a lush maze of waterways, orchards, and rice paddies known as Vietnam’s "rice bowl." Here, life revolves entirely around the water.</p>
    <p>Rather than ticking off monuments, a trip to the delta is about slowing down to match the river's pace. Hop onto a wooden sampan to glide through palm-shaded canals, sample tropical fruit straight from family orchards, and visit small workshops making coconut candy and puffed rice paper.</p>
    <p>Most travellers base themselves in Can Tho to catch the Cai Rang Floating Market at dawn, when dozens of boats raft together to trade pineapples, pumpkins, and fresh produce from tall bamboo poles. If you have extra time, push further into Ben Tre for quiet, coconut-lined waterways, or head to Chau Doc near the Cambodian border to see floating fish farms and a vibrant mix of Kinh, Khmer, and Cham cultures. Skip the rushed day trip from Ho Chi Minh City, as staying one or two nights lets you experience the delta after the tour buses leave.</p>
  </section>
